.TH GGE\-STATE 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-state \- where game state lives and why
.SH DESCRIPTION
Three kinds of state, three homes.
.SS Gameplay state: GameState
Everything a save must remember: positions, health, inventory, flags,
timers, the RNG seed, entity arrays. One pointer\-free struct, composed of
per\-mechanic structs (Player, Cat, Expedition ...), pointed to by
.B EngineConfig.user
and reached from any callback as
.BR "GameState *g = e->user" .
The engine never reads it. Because it holds no pointers, a save is
.BR memcpy :
.BR save_write (3)
and
.BR save_load (3)
take
.BR "sizeof(GameState)" .
Refer to entities by index, never by pointer.
.SS Presentation state: scene statics
How a scene looks right now: menu highlight, camera, tweens, fonts, loaded
sprites, the previous\-tick positions used for interpolation. File\-level
.B static
variables in the scene's .c, reset in
.BR on_enter .
.SS Resources: engine caches
Textures are cached by
.BR assets_texture (3)
and freed at shutdown. Sounds are owned by whoever called
.BR audio_load (3).
.SS The test
Quick\-save with
.BR "snapshot = *g" ,
play, restore with
.BR "*g = snapshot" .
Anything that did not come back is in the wrong place.
.SS Randomness
.B GameState.rng
is a uint64_t seed; every
.BR rng_range (3)
call takes its address. Seed once at new game (from the clock, or a constant
for repeatable testing) and never again. Never roll from
.B render
or
.BR handle_input ;
they run at display rate and would desynchronise saves.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gge\-scenes (7),
.BR gge\-saves (7),
.BR rng_range (3),
.BR save_write (3)
